---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import PageHero from '../components/PageHero.astro';
import Seam from '../components/Seam.astro';
import PhotoSlot from '../components/PhotoSlot.astro';
import Cta from '../components/Cta.astro';
import { site } from '../data/site';

const standards = [
  ['The reveal is even along its whole length', 'Not just at the corners, where it is easy, and not just on install day.'],
  ['Everything has a manual override', 'Reachable without demolition, documented in writing, demonstrated at handoff.'],
  ['The second option is written down', 'Including its price, so you can see the decision instead of inheriting it.'],
  ['The mechanism is cycled under load before it ships', 'Failures belong on a bench in Ephrata, not in a finished ceiling.'],
  ['One person is accountable', 'Millwork, mechanism, wiring and finish. There is nobody else to point at.'],
];
---

<Base
  title="About — one shop, four trades | Invisible Function"
  description="A one-person shop in Ephrata, PA doing all four parts of a concealment job: millwork, mechanism, low-voltage wiring and the finish work around it."
>
  <PageHero
    eyebrow="About"
    lines={['One person,', 'four trades,', 'no badge']}
    lead="Invisible Function is a shop in Ephrata, Pennsylvania that does one narrow thing: it makes equipment disappear into buildings, and takes responsibility for every part of that — the millwork, the mechanism, the wiring and the finish work."
  />

  <!-- ── Why it exists ────────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
  <section class="section surface-paper">
    <div class="wrap">
      <Seam />
      <div class="rail" style="margin-top:clamp(2rem,4vw,3.5rem)">
        <p class="label rail__label">Why</p>
        <div>
          <div data-rv="lift"><h2 class="h2">This trade has a seam running through it</h2></div>
          <div class="prose" style="margin-top:1.2rem">
            <p>
              A concealed television is four trades. A cabinet shop builds the case.
              An integrator supplies the lift and pulls the wire. An electrician gets
              power to it. A drywall finisher closes the wall around it, usually weeks
              later, usually without having seen what is behind the surface they are
              skimming.
            </p>
            <p>
              Each of them is competent. None of them owns the result. When the panel
              binds, or the reveal runs out along its length, or a faint rectangle
              appears in the ceiling in afternoon light, the honest answer is that it
              was nobody's fault in particular — which is no use at all to the person
              who paid for it.
            </p>
            <p>
              Invisible Function exists to remove that seam by doing all four parts.
              It is a deliberately small business and it will stay small, because the
              thing being sold is that one person did the whole job.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ── Independence ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
  <section class="section surface-light">
    <div class="wrap">
      <Seam />
      <div class="rail" style="margin-top:clamp(2rem,4vw,3.5rem)">
        <p class="label rail__label">Independence</p>
        <div>
          <div data-rv="lift"><h2 class="h2">Nothing to sell you first</h2></div>
          <div class="prose" style="margin-top:1.2rem">
            <p>
              There is no dealer agreement here, no annual volume commitment, and no
              showroom full of one manufacturer's mechanisms that need moving. Auton,
              Nexus&nbsp;21, Future&nbsp;Automation, Firgelli and Sonance all make
              equipment worth specifying, in different situations, at wildly different
              prices.
            </p>
            <p>
              The practical effect is small but it decides everything: the
              specification starts at the opening and works toward a mechanism,
              rather than starting at a mechanism and working toward the opening.
              Occasionally that means telling a client the cheap actuator really is
              the right answer for their bench, and losing several thousand dollars of
              margin by saying so.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="pair" style="margin-top:clamp(2rem,4vw,3rem)">
            <PhotoSlot
              what="Portrait at the bench, working, not posed to camera. Natural light, shop in the background."
              ratio="4 / 5"
              path="/images/about/portrait.jpg"
              kicker="Portrait required"
            />
            <PhotoSlot
              what="Hands and a tool at work — a detail shot that reads as craft rather than as stock photography"
              ratio="4 / 5"
              path="/images/about/hands-detail.jpg"
            />
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ===================================================================
       OWNER BACKGROUND
       Left empty on purpose. This is the one section on the site that
       cannot be written by anyone but the owner, and inventing a career
       history would undo everything the rest of the page is arguing for.

       Fill in: trade background, training, what you built before this,
       and why concealment specifically. Three short paragraphs is plenty.
       Then delete this comment and unwrap the markup below.

       <section class="section surface-paper">
         <div class="wrap">
           <div class="rail">
             <p class="label rail__label">Background</p>
             <div class="prose">…</div>
           </div>
         </div>
       </section>
       =================================================================== -->

  <!-- ── Standards ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
  <section class="section surface-dark">
    <div class="wrap">
      <Seam />
      <div class="rail" style="margin-top:clamp(2rem,4vw,3.5rem)">
        <p class="label rail__label label--accent">Standards</p>
        <div>
          <div data-rv="lift"><h2 class="h2">Five things that do not get traded away</h2></div>
          <p class="lead soft" style="margin-top:1.1rem;max-width:56ch">
            A new business has no track record to point at. What it can do is state
            its standards plainly enough to be held to them.
          </p>

          <dl class="spec" style="margin-top:clamp(1.75rem,3vw,2.5rem);max-width:66ch">
            {
              standards.map(([k, v]) => (
                <div class="spec__row" style="grid-template-columns:minmax(0,1fr);gap:.4rem">
                  <dt class="spec__k">{k}</dt>
                  <dd class="price__body" style="margin:0">{v}</dd>
                </div>
              ))
            }
          </dl>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ── Where ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
  <section class="section section--tight surface-light">
    <div class="wrap">
      <Seam />
      <div class="rail" style="margin-top:clamp(2rem,4vw,3.5rem)">
        <p class="label rail__label">The shop</p>
        <div>
          <div data-rv="lift"><h2 class="h2">{site.city}, Lancaster County</h2></div>
          <p style="margin-top:1.1rem;max-width:62ch">
            The workshop is in {site.city}, which puts Lancaster, Lititz, Hershey,
            York, Harrisburg and Reading inside a comfortable round trip. That
            matters more than it sounds: this trade involves a second visit for
            adjustment months after the invoice is paid, and a business that cannot
            afford to make that drive quietly stops making it.
          </p>
          <p class="mono soft" style="margin-top:1.4rem">
            {site.city}, {site.regionName} {site.postalCode} &nbsp;·&nbsp; {site.hours}
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <Cta
    title="Easiest way to judge a new shop is to give it something awkward"
    body="The ceiling nobody wants to open. The lift that has already been returned once. The opening that was framed to the wrong dimension."
    secondary={{ href: '/for-trade/', label: 'Trade terms' }}
  />
</Base>
